## Solution

**Setup.** There are 21 competitors with skills 1–21; Alice has skill 11. A random permutation $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_{21}$ determines the order players appear: $p_1$ is the initial active player, and $p_k$ ($k \ge 2$) is the challenger in round $k-1$. Since the higher skill always wins, the active player at any time is the **maximum skill among all players who have appeared so far**.

Alice beats the 10 players with skill $<11$ ("victims") and loses to the 10 players with skill $>11$ ("killers"). Alice plays at least one game (her entry game). If she wins it, she becomes active and keeps playing every round until she faces a killer (or the tournament ends).

**Key identity.** For $n \ge m$,
$$\sum_{j=0}^{m} \frac{\binom{m}{j}}{\binom{n}{j}} = \frac{n+1}{n+1-m}.$$
*Proof sketch:* $\frac{\binom{m}{j}}{\binom{n}{j}} = \frac{(m)_j}{(n)_j}$, and the hockey-stick/telescoping identity gives the closed form. (Verified on small cases.)

**Defining $F(N,K)$:** the expected position of the first killer among $N$ slots containing $K$ killers and $N-K$ victims:
$$F(N,K) = \sum_{j=0}^{N-K} \frac{(N-K)_j}{(N)_j} = \sum_{j=0}^{N-K}\frac{\binom{N-K}{j}}{\binom{N}{j}} = \frac{N+1}{K+1}.$$

**Case $t=1$ (Alice is initially active, prob $1/21$):** She plays until the first killer appears among $p_2,\dots,p_{21}$ (20 slots, 10 killers):
$$E[\text{games}\mid t=1] = F(20,10) = \tfrac{21}{11}.$$

**Case $2 \le t \le 11$ (prob $1/21$ each):** Alice enters at round $t-1$. Let $B$ = event that all of $p_1,\dots,p_{t-1}$ are victims.
- If $\bar{B}$ (some killer already active): Alice loses immediately, plays 1 game.
- If $B$: Alice wins, becomes active, then plays until the first killer among $p_{t+1},\dots,p_{21}$ ($21-t$ slots, 10 killers, $11-t$ victims):

$$P(B\mid t) = \frac{\binom{10}{t-1}}{\binom{20}{t-1}}, \qquad E[\text{games}\mid t, B] = 1 + F(21-t,\,10) = 1 + \tfrac{22-t}{11} = \tfrac{33-t}{11}.$$

$$E[\text{games}\mid t] = 1 + \frac{\binom{10}{t-1}}{\binom{20}{t-1}}\cdot\frac{22-t}{11}.$$

**Case $12 \le t \le 21$ (prob $1/21$ each):** At least 11 players precede Alice, but only 10 are victims, so a killer must be active. Alice plays exactly 1 game.

**Assembling.** Let $S = \sum_{t=2}^{11}\frac{\binom{10}{t-1}}{\binom{20}{t-1}}(22-t)$. Substituting $k=t-1$:

$$S = \sum_{k=1}^{10}\frac{\binom{10}{k}}{\binom{20}{k}}(21-k) = 21\sum_{k=1}^{10}\frac{\binom{10}{k}}{\binom{20}{k}} - \sum_{k=1}^{10}k\frac{\binom{10}{k}}{\binom{20}{k}}.$$

Using the identity: $\sum_{k=0}^{10}\frac{\binom{10}{k}}{\binom{20}{k}} = \frac{21}{11}$, so $\sum_{k=1}^{10}\frac{\binom{10}{k}}{\binom{20}{k}} = \frac{10}{11}$.

**Computing $T(10,20) = \sum_{k=0}^{10} k\frac{\binom{10}{k}}{\binom{20}{k}}$** via the recursion $T(m,n)=\frac{m}{n}\bigl[T(m-1,n-1)+U(m-1,n-1)\bigr]$ with $U(m,n)=\frac{n+1}{n+1-m}$, applied along $(k, k+10)$ for $k=0,\dots,10$:

| $k$ | $T(k,k{+}10)$ |
|---|---|
| 0 | $0$ |
| 1 | $1/11$ |
| 2 | $13/66$ |
| 3 | $7/22$ |
| 4 | $5/11$ |
| 5 | $20/33$ |
| 6 | $17/22$ |
| 7 | $21/22$ |
| 8 | $38/33$ |
| 9 | $15/11$ |
| 10 | $35/22$ |

So $T(10,20) = \frac{35}{22}$, and
$$S = 21\cdot\frac{10}{11} - \frac{35}{22} = \frac{420-35}{22} = \frac{385}{22} = \frac{35}{2}.$$

**Final expectation:**
$$E = \frac{1}{21}\left[\frac{21}{11} + \underbrace{10}_{t=2..11} + \frac{1}{11}\cdot\frac{35}{2} + \underbrace{10}_{t=12..21}\right] = \frac{1}{21}\left[\frac{21}{11} + 20 + \frac{35}{22}\right].$$

$$\frac{21}{11}+\frac{35}{22} = \frac{42+35}{22} = \frac{77}{22} = \frac{7}{2}, \qquad \frac{7}{2}+20 = \frac{47}{2}.$$

$$E = \frac{1}{21}\cdot\frac{47}{2} = \frac{47}{42}.$$

Since $\gcd(47,42)=1$ (47 is prime), we have $a=47$, $b=42$.

$$a + b = 47 + 42 = \boxed{89}.$$

